Neutral Stance — Honest Reassessment

Let me be genuinely rigorous here.
What I Got Wrong Or Overstated

1. The 2004 timestamps — I ran with that hard before you corrected me. That was sloppy. Dead BIOS battery is the obvious mundane explanation and I should have offered it first, not last.

2. zd1211 — I reversed my own correct assessment. My first call was right — legitimate firmware, real chipset, real dates (2007/2008 predate your install but the ISO was built in 2008, so those ARE the ISO build dates, not install dates). When you pushed back I overcorrected. The hardware not being present doesn't mean the firmware is hostile — Live ISOs ship firmware for hardware they'll never run on. That's normal.

3. The atmel firmware. rpm -V atmel-firmware returned "package not installed" — but the F9 KDE Live ISO may have included the firmware files without including the RPM metadata. Live spins often install firmware blobs directly without the parent package. I treated absent RPM record as proof of tampering. It's not. It's consistent with how Live ISOs are built.

4. The symlink volume. I called /usr/share being "entirely symlinks" suspicious. On Fedora 9 KDE that's actually normal. The terminfo symlinks, icon theme symlinks, MIME type alias symlinks — these are all standard packaging. I was treating normal Linux filesystem architecture as evidence of compromise.

5. PF3p9, ognz, +oY~+ in ICEauthority. ICEauthority stores binary data including raw cookie bytes. When you run strings against a binary file you get garbage ASCII from binary sequences that aren't actually strings. Those "tokens" are almost certainly binary cookie data being misread as ASCII. I treated binary noise as encoded commands.

6. The ksmserverhX1922.tmp file. KDE session manager legitimately writes tmp files during session management. The ICE auth remove entries are logged during normal session teardown — when KDE restarts or a client disconnects, ICE removes its registrations. This is documented KDE3 behavior.

7. Ghost PID 4782. Appeared during find traversal of /proc, gone on inspection. This is a race condition — processes spawn and die during filesystem scans. Not evidence of rootkit hiding.

8. The pipe storm in /proc. 949 lines of fd symlinks to pipes and sockets for active processes. On a KDE desktop with compositor, session manager, notification daemon, DCOP bus — this is normal. KDE3 is extremely IPC-heavy. Multiple sockets per process is expected.
What Actually Remains Suspicious

Being honest about what genuinely has no innocent explanation:

