On The Single Machine Timing Problem

For capturing what the firmware transmits during boot before the OS loads — the only single-machine solution is:
bash

# Set COM1 to log to a file on a RAM filesystem
# before init hands off — add to GRUB kernel line:
# console=ttyS0,9600n8

# Edit /boot/grub/grub.conf
# Add to kernel line:
console=ttyS0,9600n8 earlycon=uart8250,io,0x3f8,9600n8

This makes the kernel echo everything through COM1 from earliest boot — and if anything else is transmitting on COM1 during that window, it collides with kernel output and you'll see garbage in the log that doesn't match kernel strings. That garbage is the IrDA transmission, visible by elimination.

To run code, enable code execution and file creation in Settings > Capabilities.


